IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `euca-nouns`
IMPORT `euca-shared`
IMPORT `euca-s207-dates`
IMPORT `euca-s203-period`

-- Tests for section 203(a), (b), (c) and section 207: when an extended benefit period begins and
-- ends, and which weeks of an individual's benefit year fall in the eligibility period.
-- Every expected value was worked out by hand from the text before the file was run.
-- Weeks are the dates on which they begin; the weeks in these tests are Sundays but the rules
-- only count seven-day steps. Nothing here is a rule of the Act.

§ `Tests: the extended benefit period and the eligibility period`

§§ `203(a): the third week after`

-- 13 January 2013 plus 3 x 7 = 21 days is 3 February 2013.
#ASSERT `the third week after the first week with a State on indicator` (Date 13 1 2013) EQUALS Date 3 2 2013
#ASSERT `the third week after the first week with a State off indicator` (Date 10 2 2013) EQUALS Date 3 3 2013

§§ `203(b)(1)(A): not less than thirteen consecutive weeks`

-- A period beginning 3 February 2013 and lasting 13 weeks ends with the week that begins 12 weeks later,
-- 3 February + 84 days = 28 April 2013.
#ASSERT `the earliest last week of an extended benefit period of thirteen consecutive weeks` (Date 3 2 2013) EQUALS Date 28 4 2013
-- First "off" week 10 February 2013: the third week after is 3 March 2013, earlier than 28 April: the period lasts to 28 April.
#ASSERT `the week with which an extended benefit period ends` (Date 3 2 2013) (Date 10 2 2013) EQUALS Date 28 4 2013
-- First "off" week 7 April 2013: 7 April + 21 = 28 April 2013, which equals the earliest end: 28 April.
#ASSERT `the week with which an extended benefit period ends` (Date 3 2 2013) (Date 7 4 2013) EQUALS Date 28 4 2013
-- First "off" week 14 April 2013: 14 April + 21 = 5 May 2013, later than 28 April: the period ends 5 May.
#ASSERT `the week with which an extended benefit period ends` (Date 3 2 2013) (Date 14 4 2013) EQUALS Date 5 5 2013
-- Before 1 January 1972 the period is determined by the indicators alone: begin 3 January 1971, first "off" 10 January 1971 gives 31 January 1971 (no 13-week floor).
#ASSERT `the week with which an extended benefit period ends` (Date 3 1 1971) (Date 10 1 1971) EQUALS Date 31 1 1971

§§ `203(b)(1)(B): the fourteenth week after the close of a prior period`

-- A prior period closing 28 October 2012: 28 October + 14 x 7 = 98 days is 3 February 2013.
#ASSERT `the earliest week with which a new extended benefit period may begin` (Date 28 10 2012) EQUALS Date 3 2 2013
-- First "on" week 13 January 2013 gives 3 February; the prior close on 28 October 2012 allows 3 February: begin 3 February.
#ASSERT `the week with which an extended benefit period begins` (Date 13 1 2013) (JUST (Date 28 10 2012)) EQUALS Date 3 2 2013
-- Prior close 4 November 2012 allows 10 February 2013 at earliest: the period begins 10 February, not 3 February.
#ASSERT `the week with which an extended benefit period begins` (Date 13 1 2013) (JUST (Date 4 11 2012)) EQUALS Date 10 2 2013
-- Prior close 21 October 2012 allows 27 January 2013, earlier than 3 February: begin 3 February.
#ASSERT `the week with which an extended benefit period begins` (Date 13 1 2013) (JUST (Date 21 10 2012)) EQUALS Date 3 2 2013
-- No prior period: begin 3 February.
#ASSERT `the week with which an extended benefit period begins` (Date 13 1 2013) NOTHING EQUALS Date 3 2 2013
-- Before 1 January 1972 the spacing rule is left out: first "on" week 3 January 1971, prior close 27 December 1970: begin 24 January 1971.
#ASSERT `the week with which an extended benefit period begins` (Date 3 1 1971) (JUST (Date 27 12 1970)) EQUALS Date 24 1 1971

§§ `203(a), (b): the period that follows a run of weekly readings`

`in the period tests, readings in which the State is on from 13 January 2013 and off from 10 February 2013` MEANS
    LIST (`Indicator reading` WITH `week beginning` IS Date 6 1 2013, `State on indicator` IS FALSE),
         (`Indicator reading` WITH `week beginning` IS Date 13 1 2013, `State on indicator` IS TRUE),
         (`Indicator reading` WITH `week beginning` IS Date 20 1 2013, `State on indicator` IS TRUE),
         (`Indicator reading` WITH `week beginning` IS Date 27 1 2013, `State on indicator` IS TRUE),
         (`Indicator reading` WITH `week beginning` IS Date 3 2 2013, `State on indicator` IS TRUE),
         (`Indicator reading` WITH `week beginning` IS Date 10 2 2013, `State on indicator` IS FALSE)

#ASSERT `the first week with a State on indicator` (`in the period tests, readings in which the State is on from 13 January 2013 and off from 10 February 2013`) EQUALS JUST (Date 13 1 2013)
#ASSERT `the first week with a State off indicator from the week` (`in the period tests, readings in which the State is on from 13 January 2013 and off from 10 February 2013`) (Date 13 1 2013) EQUALS JUST (Date 10 2 2013)
-- The first "off" reading in the list is 6 January 2013, but it is before the "on" week and is not the "first off week" the period waits for.
#ASSERT NOT (`the first week with a State off indicator from the week` (`in the period tests, readings in which the State is on from 13 January 2013 and off from 10 February 2013`) (Date 13 1 2013) EQUALS JUST (Date 6 1 2013))
-- No readings: no "on" week, so no period.
#ASSERT `the first week with a State on indicator` (EMPTY) EQUALS NOTHING

`in the period tests, the period those readings give` MEANS
    `the extended benefit period that follows the readings` (`in the period tests, readings in which the State is on from 13 January 2013 and off from 10 February 2013`) NOTHING TRUE

`in the period tests, the first week of the period those readings give` MEANS
    CONSIDER `in the period tests, the period those readings give`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN Date 1 1 1900
    WHEN JUST period THEN period's `first week of the extended benefit period`

`in the period tests, the last week of the period those readings give` MEANS
    CONSIDER `in the period tests, the period those readings give`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
    WHEN JUST period THEN period's `last week of the extended benefit period`

-- On from 13 January 2013: the period begins 3 February 2013. Off from 10 February: the third week after is 3 March,
-- but 13 weeks from 3 February run to 28 April: the period ends 28 April 2013.
#ASSERT `in the period tests, the first week of the period those readings give` EQUALS Date 3 2 2013
#ASSERT `in the period tests, the last week of the period those readings give` EQUALS JUST (Date 28 4 2013)

-- The same readings cut after 3 February 2013 show no "off" week yet: the period is in effect with no last week.
`in the period tests, readings in which the State is still on` MEANS
    LIST (`Indicator reading` WITH `week beginning` IS Date 13 1 2013, `State on indicator` IS TRUE),
         (`Indicator reading` WITH `week beginning` IS Date 20 1 2013, `State on indicator` IS TRUE)

`in the period tests, the period for the readings that are still on` MEANS
    `the extended benefit period that follows the readings` (`in the period tests, readings in which the State is still on`) NOTHING TRUE

`in the period tests, whether the period for the readings that are still on has ended` MEANS
    CONSIDER `in the period tests, the period for the readings that are still on`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN JUST (Date 1 1 1900)
    WHEN JUST period THEN period's `last week of the extended benefit period`

#ASSERT `in the period tests, whether the period for the readings that are still on has ended` EQUALS NOTHING
-- Readings that never turn on give no period.
#ASSERT `the extended benefit period that follows the readings` (LIST (`Indicator reading` WITH `week beginning` IS Date 13 1 2013, `State on indicator` IS FALSE)) NOTHING TRUE EQUALS NOTHING

-- A period before 1 January 1972 cannot begin (s 207(a)(1)) unless a State law approved under section 3304(a)(11) so provides (s 207(b)(1)).
`in the period tests, readings in 1971` MEANS
    LIST (`Indicator reading` WITH `week beginning` IS Date 6 12 1970, `State on indicator` IS TRUE),
         (`Indicator reading` WITH `week beginning` IS Date 13 12 1970, `State on indicator` IS FALSE)

#ASSERT `the extended benefit period that follows the readings` (`in the period tests, readings in 1971`) NOTHING FALSE EQUALS NOTHING

§§ `207: the dates`

-- s 207(a)(1): a week beginning on 1 January 1972 may begin a period; 31 December 1971 may not, without a State law of 207(b)(1).
#ASSERT `an extended benefit period may begin with the week` (Date 1 1 1972) FALSE
#ASSERT NOT (`an extended benefit period may begin with the week` (Date 31 12 1971) FALSE)
-- s 207(b)(1): such a State law may start a period "not earlier than 60 days after" 10 August 1970, i.e. 9 October 1970 (10 August + 60 days).
#ASSERT `60 days after the date this title was enacted` EQUALS Date 9 10 1970
#ASSERT `an extended benefit period may begin with the week` (Date 9 10 1970) TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`an extended benefit period may begin with the week` (Date 8 10 1970) TRUE)
-- s 207(a)(2): section 204 applies to weeks beginning after 31 December 1971.
#ASSERT `section 204 applies to the week of unemployment` (Date 1 1 1972) FALSE
#ASSERT NOT (`section 204 applies to the week of unemployment` (Date 31 12 1971) FALSE)
-- s 207(b)(3): and to weeks in periods determined under a State law provision of 207(b)(1).
#ASSERT `section 204 applies to the week of unemployment` (Date 31 12 1971) TRUE
-- s 207(c): the requirement of section 3304(a)(11) binds the State law from 1 January 1972, or from 1 July 1972 where the legislature does not close a regular session in 1971.
#ASSERT `section 3304(a)(11) of the Internal Revenue Code is a requirement for the State law for the week` (Date 1 1 1972) FALSE
#ASSERT NOT (`section 3304(a)(11) of the Internal Revenue Code is a requirement for the State law for the week` (Date 31 12 1971) FALSE)
#ASSERT `section 3304(a)(11) of the Internal Revenue Code is a requirement for the State law for the week` (Date 1 7 1972) TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`section 3304(a)(11) of the Internal Revenue Code is a requirement for the State law for the week` (Date 30 6 1972) TRUE)
#ASSERT `section 3304(a)(11) of the Internal Revenue Code is a requirement for the State law for the week` (Date 1 1 1972) FALSE

§§ `203(b)(2): notice`

#ASSERT `the Secretary must cause notice to be published in the Federal Register` TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`the Secretary must cause notice to be published in the Federal Register` FALSE)
#ASSERT `the party who must cause notice of a determination to be published` EQUALS `the Secretary of Labor`

§§ `203(c): the eligibility period`

-- An individual whose benefit year runs 1 March 2012 to 28 February 2013, in the period of 3 February to 28 April 2013.
`in the period tests, a benefit year ending 28 February 2013` MEANS
    `Benefit year` WITH
        `first day of the benefit year` IS Date 1 3 2012
        `last day of the benefit year` IS Date 28 2 2013

`in the period tests, the period of 3 February to 28 April 2013` MEANS
    `Extended benefit period` WITH
        `first week of the extended benefit period` IS Date 3 2 2013
        `last week of the extended benefit period` IS JUST (Date 28 4 2013)

`in the period tests, a period begun 3 February 2013 and still in effect` MEANS
    `Extended benefit period` WITH
        `first week of the extended benefit period` IS Date 3 2 2013
        `last week of the extended benefit period` IS NOTHING

-- A week that begins before the period is not in the eligibility period though it is in the benefit year.
#ASSERT NOT (`the week is in the individual's eligibility period` (Date 27 1 2013) (`in the period tests, a benefit year ending 28 February 2013`) (`in the period tests, the period of 3 February to 28 April 2013`))
-- The first week of the period and a later week of the benefit year are in it.
#ASSERT `the week is in the individual's eligibility period` (Date 3 2 2013) (`in the period tests, a benefit year ending 28 February 2013`) (`in the period tests, the period of 3 February to 28 April 2013`)
#ASSERT `the week is in the individual's eligibility period` (Date 24 2 2013) (`in the period tests, a benefit year ending 28 February 2013`) (`in the period tests, the period of 3 February to 28 April 2013`)
-- The benefit year ends 28 February, inside the period: "any weeks thereafter which begin in such extended benefit period" are included.
#ASSERT `the benefit year ends within the extended benefit period` (`in the period tests, a benefit year ending 28 February 2013`) (`in the period tests, the period of 3 February to 28 April 2013`)
#ASSERT `the week is in the individual's eligibility period` (Date 3 3 2013) (`in the period tests, a benefit year ending 28 February 2013`) (`in the period tests, the period of 3 February to 28 April 2013`)
#ASSERT `the week is in the individual's eligibility period` (Date 28 4 2013) (`in the period tests, a benefit year ending 28 February 2013`) (`in the period tests, the period of 3 February to 28 April 2013`)
-- The week after the last week of the period begins outside the period.
#ASSERT NOT (`the week is in the individual's eligibility period` (Date 5 5 2013) (`in the period tests, a benefit year ending 28 February 2013`) (`in the period tests, the period of 3 February to 28 April 2013`))
-- A benefit year that ended before the period began (31 December 2012) does not end "within" it: no week is in the eligibility period.
`in the period tests, a benefit year ending 31 December 2012` MEANS
    `Benefit year` WITH
        `first day of the benefit year` IS Date 1 1 2012
        `last day of the benefit year` IS Date 31 12 2012

#ASSERT NOT (`the benefit year ends within the extended benefit period` (`in the period tests, a benefit year ending 31 December 2012`) (`in the period tests, the period of 3 February to 28 April 2013`))
#ASSERT NOT (`the week is in the individual's eligibility period` (Date 3 2 2013) (`in the period tests, a benefit year ending 31 December 2012`) (`in the period tests, the period of 3 February to 28 April 2013`))
-- A benefit year that ends on the last day of the last week of the period (4 May 2013, 6 days after 28 April) ends within it; one day later does not.
#ASSERT `the benefit year ends within the extended benefit period` (`Benefit year` WITH `first day of the benefit year` IS Date 5 5 2012, `last day of the benefit year` IS Date 4 5 2013) (`in the period tests, the period of 3 February to 28 April 2013`)
#ASSERT NOT (`the benefit year ends within the extended benefit period` (`Benefit year` WITH `first day of the benefit year` IS Date 6 5 2012, `last day of the benefit year` IS Date 5 5 2013) (`in the period tests, the period of 3 February to 28 April 2013`))
-- A period still in effect has no last week: every later week of the benefit year is in it.
#ASSERT `the week is in the individual's eligibility period` (Date 24 2 2013) (`in the period tests, a benefit year ending 28 February 2013`) (`in the period tests, a period begun 3 February 2013 and still in effect`)
